\title{Order Parameters and Critical Thresholds:\\ Two Exactly-Solvable Mean-Field Phase Transitions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a rigorous, self-contained analysis of the paradigmatic \emph{second-order
phase transition} of statistical mechanics---the spontaneous magnetization of the
mean-field (Curie--Weiss) Ising ferromagnet---and of its combinatorial cousin, the
emergence of a giant connected component in mean-field percolation (equivalently,
the survival of a Poisson Galton--Watson branching process). In each case the model
reduces to a scalar self-consistency equation for an \emph{order parameter}: the
magnetization $m = \tanh(\beta m)$ and the survival probability
$\rho = 1 - e^{-\lambda \rho}$. We prove that both models undergo a phase
transition at the critical value $1$ of their coupling: below threshold the only
solution is the trivial one, while above threshold a nontrivial ordered branch
appears. We further establish quantitative lower bounds near criticality---%
$m^2 \ge 3(\beta-1)/\beta^3$ and $\rho \ge 2(\lambda-1)/\lambda^2$---which exhibit
the continuous (second-order) onset of order and identify the mean-field critical
exponents $\tfrac12$ (magnetization) and $1$ (percolation). The two different
exponents, arising from the same structural template, illustrate universality. All
results are proved from elementary Taylor-type inequalities for $\tanh$ and
$1 - e^{-x}$ derived from first principles.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{phase transition} is a qualitative change in the macroscopic state of a
many-body system induced by the smooth variation of a control parameter across a
critical value. The organizing concept is the \emph{order parameter}: a scalar
that vanishes in the disordered phase and becomes nonzero in the ordered phase. A
transition is \emph{first order} when the order parameter jumps discontinuously and
\emph{second order} when it grows continuously from zero, typically as a power
$(g - g_c)^{\beta_{\mathrm{exp}}}$ of the distance from the critical coupling; the
exponent $\beta_{\mathrm{exp}}$ is a \emph{critical exponent}.

We treat two exactly-solvable models:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The mean-field (Curie--Weiss) Ising ferromagnet.} In zero external
  field the average magnetization $m$ satisfies $m = \tanh(\beta m)$, with $\beta$
  the (rescaled) inverse temperature.
  \item \textbf{Mean-field percolation / the Poisson branching process.} In the
  Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi random graph $G(n, \lambda/n)$, the survival probability
  $\rho$---asymptotically the giant-component fraction---satisfies
  $\rho = 1 - e^{-\lambda\rho}$, the extinction--survival equation of a
  $\Poisson(\lambda)$ Galton--Watson tree.
\end{enumerate}
Both are governed by a scalar self-consistency equation $x = \Phi(x)$ with $\Phi$
smooth, concave on $[0,\infty)$, fixing $0$, and with $\Phi'(0)$ equal to the
coupling; a transition occurs precisely when $\Phi'(0)$ crosses $1$.

\section{Definitions and setup}

All variables are real.

\begin{definition}[Magnetization]
For a coupling $\beta$, a real number $m$ is a \emph{magnetization} if
$\tanh(\beta m) = m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Survival probability]
For a mean connectivity $\lambda$, a real number $\rho$ is a \emph{survival
probability} if $\rho = 1 - e^{-\lambda \rho}$.
\end{definition}

In both cases $x=0$ is a fixed point for every coupling; a phase transition is the
appearance of a nontrivial fixed point. For both maps $\Phi'(0)$ equals the
coupling, so the critical values are $\beta_c = 1$ and $\lambda_c = 1$.

\section{Analytic toolkit}

\subsection{The hyperbolic tangent}

\begin{lemma}[Derivative]\label{lem:dtanh}
For all $x$, $\dfrac{d}{dx}\tanh x = 1 - \tanh^2 x$; in particular $\tanh$ is
differentiable and continuous everywhere.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $\tanh = \sinh/\cosh$ and apply the quotient rule with $\sinh' = \cosh$,
$\cosh' = \sinh$ and $\cosh^2 - \sinh^2 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sub-diagonal bound]\label{lem:tanhlt}
For every $x > 0$, $\tanh x < x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equivalently $\sinh x < x\cosh x$. The function $g(t) = t\cosh t - \sinh t$
satisfies $g(0) = 0$ and $g'(t) = t\sinh t > 0$ for $t>0$, so $g$ is strictly
increasing on $[0,\infty)$ and $g(x) > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonnegativity]\label{lem:tanhnn}
For $x \ge 0$, $\tanh x \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Cubic Taylor lower bound]\label{lem:tanhcubic}
For $x \ge 0$, $\tanh x \ge x - \dfrac{x^3}{3}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $h(t) = \tanh t - (t - t^3/3)$. Then $h(0) = 0$ and, by
Lemma~\ref{lem:dtanh}, $h'(t) = (1 - \tanh^2 t) - (1 - t^2) = t^2 - \tanh^2 t$.
For $t \ge 0$, $0 \le \tanh t \le t$ (Lemmas~\ref{lem:tanhlt}--\ref{lem:tanhnn}),
so $h'(t) \ge 0$ and $h$ is nondecreasing; hence $h(x) \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

We also use $|\tanh x| < 1$ and $\tanh(-x) = -\tanh x$.

\subsection{The percolation nonlinearity}

\begin{lemma}[Sub-diagonal bound]\label{lem:explt}
For every $x > 0$, $1 - e^{-x} < x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The strict convexity bound $1 + t < e^{t}$ ($t \ne 0$) at $t = -x$ gives
$1 - x < e^{-x}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Quadratic Taylor lower bound]\label{lem:expquad}
For $x \ge 0$, $1 - e^{-x} \ge x - \dfrac{x^2}{2}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $k(t) = (1 - t + t^2/2) - e^{-t}$. Then $k(0) = 0$ and
$k'(t) = t + e^{-t} - 1 \ge 0$ by $e^{-t} \ge 1 - t$; hence $k$ is nondecreasing,
so $k(x) \ge 0$, which rearranges to the claim.
\end{proof}

\section{The Curie--Weiss transition}

\begin{proposition}[Trivial solution]
$m = 0$ is a magnetization for every $\beta$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Symmetry]\label{prop:sym}
If $m$ is a magnetization at coupling $\beta$, then so is $-m$, since
$\tanh(\beta(-m)) = -\tanh(\beta m) = -m$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Boundedness]
Every magnetization satisfies $|m| < 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness below threshold]\label{thm:cwsub}
If $0 < \beta \le 1$, the only magnetization is $m = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $m > 0$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:tanhlt}, $\tanh(\beta m) < \beta m \le m$,
contradicting $\tanh(\beta m) = m$. If $m < 0$, apply this to $-m$ via
Proposition~\ref{prop:sym}. Hence $m = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence above threshold]\label{thm:cwsup}
If $\beta > 1$, there exists a magnetization $m > 0$ (and hence also $-m$).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $F(m) = \tanh(\beta m) - m$, which is continuous. Put
$c = 3(\beta-1)/\beta^3 > 0$ and $a = \tfrac12\sqrt{c}$, so $a^2 = c/4$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:tanhcubic},
$\tanh(\beta a) \ge \beta a - (\beta a)^3/3 = \beta a - (\beta^3 a^2/3)a$. Since
$\beta^3 a^2 = \tfrac34(\beta-1)$, a short computation gives
$\beta a - (\beta a)^3/3 > a$, so $F(a) > 0$. Also $F(a+1) < 1 - (a+1) \le 0$ as
$\tanh < 1$. By the intermediate value theorem there is $m \in (a, a+1)$ with
$F(m) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Near-critical bound; exponent $\tfrac12$]\label{thm:cwexp}
If $\beta > 1$ and $m > 0$ is a magnetization, then
$m^2 \ge \dfrac{3(\beta - 1)}{\beta^3}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:tanhcubic}, $m = \tanh(\beta m) \ge \beta m - (\beta m)^3/3$, so
$0 \ge (\beta-1)m - \beta^3 m^3/3$; dividing by $m>0$ and $\beta^3/3$ gives the
bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
As $\beta \downarrow 1$, $m \gtrsim \sqrt{3(\beta-1)}$: the magnetization emerges
continuously with mean-field exponent $\tfrac12$. The transition is second order.
\end{corollary}

\section{The percolation / giant-component transition}

\begin{proposition}[Trivial solution and boundedness]
$\rho = 0$ is a survival probability for every $\lambda$, and every survival
probability satisfies $\rho < 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[No giant component below threshold]\label{thm:persub}
If $0 < \lambda \le 1$ and $\rho \ge 0$ is a survival probability, then $\rho = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\rho > 0$, Lemma~\ref{lem:explt} gives
$\rho = 1 - e^{-\lambda\rho} < \lambda\rho \le \rho$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Giant component above threshold]\label{thm:persup}
If $\lambda > 1$, there exists a survival probability with $0 < \rho < 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $G(\rho) = (1 - e^{-\lambda\rho}) - \rho$ and $a = (\lambda-1)/\lambda^2 \in (0,1)$,
so $\lambda a = (\lambda-1)/\lambda$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:expquad},
\[
1 - e^{-\lambda a} \ge \lambda a - \tfrac12(\lambda a)^2
= \frac{\lambda-1}{\lambda} - \frac{(\lambda-1)^2}{2\lambda^2}
> \frac{\lambda-1}{\lambda^2} = a,
\]
so $G(a) > 0$; and $G(1) = -e^{-\lambda} < 0$. The intermediate value theorem gives
$\rho \in (a, 1)$ with $G(\rho) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Near-critical bound; exponent $1$]\label{thm:perexp}
If $\lambda > 1$ and $\rho > 0$ is a survival probability, then
$\rho \ge \dfrac{2(\lambda - 1)}{\lambda^2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:expquad}, $\rho = 1 - e^{-\lambda\rho} \ge \lambda\rho - (\lambda\rho)^2/2$,
so $0 \ge (\lambda-1)\rho - \lambda^2\rho^2/2$; dividing by $\rho>0$ and
$\lambda^2/2$ gives the bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
As $\lambda \downarrow 1$, $\rho \gtrsim 2(\lambda-1)$: the giant-component fraction
grows linearly, with mean-field percolation exponent $1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Universality: one template, two exponents}

Both models share the template of a self-consistency map $\Phi$ with $\Phi(0)=0$,
concave and increasing on $[0,\infty)$, and $\Phi'(0)$ equal to the coupling; the
transition occurs exactly when $\Phi'(0)$ crosses $1$, giving $\beta_c = \lambda_c = 1$.
The onset, however, is dictated by the first nonlinear term:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\tanh(\beta m) = \beta m - \tfrac13(\beta m)^3 + \cdots$ is cubic (the
  $m \mapsto -m$ symmetry forbids a quadratic term), so balancing $(\beta-1)m$
  against $m^3$ gives $m \sim \sqrt{\beta-1}$, exponent $\tfrac12$.
  \item $1 - e^{-\lambda\rho} = \lambda\rho - \tfrac12(\lambda\rho)^2 + \cdots$ is
  quadratic (no sign symmetry), so balancing $(\lambda-1)\rho$ against $\rho^2$
  gives $\rho \sim \lambda - 1$, exponent $1$.
\end{itemize}
That the same skeleton yields distinct exponents governed only by symmetry and the
leading nonlinearity is an exactly-solvable instance of universality.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The Curie--Weiss result is the mean-field backbone of ferromagnetism and
order--disorder transitions. The percolation result underlies the Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi
giant-component threshold, the epidemic outbreak criterion $R_0 > 1$, and cascading
network failures. As a speculative program, one may model a body of mathematical
results as a graph whose vertices are theorems and whose edges are logical or
conceptual links; a coherence order parameter---the fraction of results in the
largest component---would then, by the branching heuristic, remain near zero until
the average number of links per result crosses $1$, at which point a single
connected theory percolates. The rigorous results above supply the scaffolding such
a program would rest on.

\section{Discussion and future work}

We proved lower bounds near criticality. Natural next steps are: (1) matching upper
bounds to pin the exponents $\tfrac12$ and $1$ exactly; (2) uniqueness and
monotonicity of the ordered branch via strict concavity; (3) a free-energy
variational formulation with $f(m) = -\tfrac12 m^2 - \beta^{-1}\log\cosh(\beta m)$;
(4) the passage from mean field to Bernoulli percolation on trees and, ultimately,
$\mathbb{Z}^d$; and (5) a discrete companion showing that a simple graph on $n$
vertices with more than $\binom{n-1}{2}$ edges is connected (sharp via
$K_{n-1} \sqcup \{\mathrm{pt}\}$).

\section{Conclusion}

The scalar equations $m = \tanh(\beta m)$ and $\rho = 1 - e^{-\lambda\rho}$ capture
two of science's most important transitions. Each undergoes a sharp transition at
coupling $1$: no order below, a continuously emerging ordered branch above, with
mean-field critical exponents $\tfrac12$ and $1$. The shared template and divergent
exponents form a transparent, fully rigorous window onto criticality and
universality.

